---
<QuestionLayout
  title="Why does model.execute return unauthorized_output when my host passes model evidence through?"
  description="Traverse's host-private output filter rejects strings with :// or a leading /, and the full ModelRightsRecord carries source URLs. model.execute@1.1.0 now defines a normative flat model_evidence shape without URLs; emit that."
  canonical="https://traverse-framework.com/questions/why-does-model-execute-return-unauthorized-output.html"
  category="Runtime and architecture"
  relatedLinks={relatedLinks}
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <p><strong>Short answer:</strong> the runtime is protecting the guest from host-private data, and the full rights record has URLs in it. Traverse's connector output filter rejects any string that starts with <code>/</code> or contains <code>://</code>. If your bridge forwards the host's whole <code>ModelRightsRecord</code> into <a href="/questions/what-is-the-model-execute-catalog-capability.html"><code>model.execute@1.0.0</code></a>, its <code>source_url</code> fields trip that filter and the call fails with <code>unauthorized_output</code> (<code>-4</code>).</p>

  <h2>Why the filter is there</h2>
  <p>A capability guest shouldn't see host paths or endpoints. That's part of the mediated connector rules in Spec 104: the host does the I/O, and only checked, host-neutral data crosses back into the WASM. Paths and URLs are the easiest leak to catch, so the native executor refuses them outright rather than guessing which ones are safe.</p>

  <h2>What the record looks like</h2>
  <p>Both Spec 138 hosts, native <code>traverse_runtime::exact_model</code> and the web <code>ExactModelBrowserHost</code>, return a nested record: model id, version and digest, a <code>rights</code> object (license, commercial use, attribution, redistribution, <code>source_url</code>, and a derivation block that can also carry a URL), plus <code>status</code> and <code>effective_usage</code>. The catalog contract's example shows a flat <code>{"{"} license_id, commercial_use {"}"}</code>, so the two don't line up exactly.</p>

  <h2>What to do now</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Emit the normative shape.</strong> Since 2026-10-29 the registry carries <code>model.execute@1.1.0</code> (<a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/registry/issues/641">registry#641</a>, closed by <a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/registry/pull/645">registry#645</a>). Its contract defines a flat <code>model_evidence</code> object: <code>license_id</code>, <code>commercial_use</code>, <code>status</code>, <code>effective_usage</code>, and an optional <code>derivation</code> with no URLs in it. Build exactly that in your bridge and leave the rest of the rights record on the host side.</li>
    <li><strong>Don't forward the full record.</strong> The 1.1.0 contract states that passing the nested <code>ModelRightsRecord</code> through is non-conformant under Spec 104, so <code>unauthorized_output</code> here is expected behavior, not a bug.</li>
    <li><strong>Same WASM as 1.0.0.</strong> 1.1.0 reuses the 1.0.0 artifact (same digest); what changed is the contract. The guest still copies whatever the host hands it, so byte-identical output across hosts depends on every host emitting the same shape.</li>
    <li><strong>Still open.</strong> <a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/traverse/issues/1719">traverse#1719</a> tracks a shipped binding that links <code>connector_invoke</code> and emits this shape on the published Browser, Node and Swift hosts. Until it lands in a release, you still write the bridge yourself.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>One more caveat</h2>
  <p>The contract declares <code>additionalProperties: false</code> on inputs, but on v0.15.0 the WASM still accepts an extra field and runs. Runtime-side enforcement (traverse#1671) is on <code>main</code> and not in a release yet.</p>
</QuestionLayout>
